# Finance

Interest, annuities, loan payments, present values and net present value. Rates are per period as decimals (5% is $0.05$). Import with `![[std/finance.mpp]]`.

## Interest

Compound interest: a principal $P$ at rate $r$ per period, compounded $n$ times a period, after $t$ periods.

$$ A_{\text{compound}}(P, r, n, t) := P \left(1 + \frac{r}{n}\right)^{n t} $$

$$ A_{\text{compound}}(1000, 0.05, 1, 2) \overset{?}{=} 1102.5 $$

$$ A_{\text{compound}}(100, 0.12, 12, 1) \overset{?}{=} 100 \cdot 1.01^{12} $$

Continuous compounding, the limit as $n \to \infty$.

$$ A_{\text{cont}}(P, r, t) := P e^{r t} $$

$$ A_{\text{cont}}(100, \tfrac{1}{10}, 10) \overset{?}{=} 100 e $$

$$ \lim_{n \to \infty} \left(1 + \frac{1}{10 n}\right)^{10 n} \overset{?}{=} e $$

Simple interest, for comparison.

$$ A_{\text{simple}}(P, r, t) := P (1 + r t) $$

$$ A_{\text{simple}}(1000, 0.05, 2) \overset{?}{=} 1100 $$

## Present value

The value now of an amount $F$ due after $t$ periods at rate $r$, compounded per period and continuously: $PV = F e^{-rT}$ under constant continuous discounting.

$$ \mathrm{PV}_{\text{disc}}(F, r, t) := \frac{F}{(1 + r)^t} $$

$$ \mathrm{PV}_{\text{disc}}(1102.5, 0.05, 2) \overset{?}{=} 1000 $$

$$ \mathrm{PV}_{\text{cont}}(F, r, T) := F e^{-r T} $$

$$ \mathrm{PV}_{\text{cont}}(100 e, \tfrac{1}{10}, 10) \overset{?}{=} 100 $$

$$ \mathrm{PV}_{\text{cont}}(100, \tfrac{1}{20}, 2) \overset{?}{=} \operatorname{presentvalue}(100, \tfrac{1}{20}, 2) $$

## Annuities and loans

The present value of $n$ payments of $C$ at the end of each period (an ordinary annuity).

$$ \mathrm{PV}_{\text{annuity}}(C, r, n) := C \frac{1 - (1 + r)^{-n}}{r} $$

$$ \mathrm{PV}_{\text{annuity}}(100, \tfrac{1}{10}, 2) \overset{?}{=} \frac{100}{1.1} + \frac{100}{1.1^2} $$

$$ \mathrm{PV}_{\text{annuity}}(C, r, 3) \overset{?}{=} \sum_{k=1}^{3} \frac{C}{(1 + r)^k} $$

The future value of the same payments.

$$ \mathrm{FV}_{\text{annuity}}(C, r, n) := C \frac{(1 + r)^n - 1}{r} $$

$$ \mathrm{FV}_{\text{annuity}}(100, \tfrac{1}{10}, 2) \overset{?}{=} 210 $$

The payment per period that repays a loan $P$ over $n$ periods at rate $r$.

$$ M_{\text{loan}}(P, r, n) := \frac{P r}{1 - (1 + r)^{-n}} $$

$$ M_{\text{loan}}(\mathrm{PV}_{\text{annuity}}(100, \tfrac{1}{10}, 5), \tfrac{1}{10}, 5) \overset{?}{=} 100 $$

$$ M_{\text{loan}}(1000, \tfrac{1}{10}, 1) \overset{?}{=} 1100 $$

## Net present value

The net present value of an outlay $C_0$ now and returns $C_1$, $C_2$, $C_3$ at the ends of the next three periods.

$$ \mathrm{NPV}(r, C_0, C_1, C_2, C_3) := -C_0 + \frac{C_1}{1 + r} + \frac{C_2}{(1 + r)^2} + \frac{C_3}{(1 + r)^3} $$

$$ \mathrm{NPV}(\tfrac{1}{10}, 100, 0, 0, 133.1) \overset{?}{=} 0 $$

$$ \mathrm{NPV}(0, 100, 40, 40, 40) \overset{?}{=} 20 $$

## Options

European put–call parity without dividends: $C - P = S - K e^{-rT}$ for spot $S$, strike $K$ and time $T$ to expiry.

$$ P_{\text{parity}}(C, S, K, r, T) := C - S + K e^{-r T} $$

$$ P_{\text{parity}}(10, 100, 100, 0, 1) \overset{?}{=} 10 $$

$$ P_{\text{parity}}(10, 100, 105, \tfrac{1}{20}, 1) \overset{?}{=} \operatorname{putfromcall}(10, 100, 105, \tfrac{1}{20}, 1) $$
